# {{.Module}}

A starter bot built on the [Aisotropy](https://github.com/Luum-Home/aisotropy)
in-process LLM runtime, scaffolded with `aisotropy new`. It is a complete,
runnable shape — a persona, one example tool, and a browser dev chat — ready for
you to customize.

## Run it

```sh
go mod tidy          # resolve and pin the aisotropy dependency
go run .              # boots the runtime on :8080
```

Then open the built-in dev chat:

<http://localhost:8080/ui>

No API key is required: with `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` unset the runtime uses a keyless
stub client, so the loop completes and you can exercise the wiring. Set a real
key for real model responses:

```sh
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-...
go run .
```

## What you get

- **A persona** — the base system prompt in `main.go`. It is registered under the
  product_id `dev-ui`, which is exactly the product_id the dev chat sends by
  default, so the chat speaks with your persona out of the box.
- **One example tool** — `greet`, a typed tool (`greetArgs`) the model can call.
  Replace it with a real capability; the argument struct drives the model-facing
  schema, decoding, and validation all at once.
- **A dev chat at `/ui`** — a self-contained, single-page chat for local
  iteration.

## Not for production

Two things in `main.go` are development conveniences and are **not safe to
ship**:

- `WithDevUI()` mounts an **unauthenticated** single-page UI. Remove it (and
  build your own frontend on the `/chat/agui` or `/chat/aisdk` adapters) before
  you deploy.
- `WithAuthenticator(NoAuth())` trusts the tenant/user in the request path with
  no credential check. Swap in `NewJWTAuthenticator` before exposing the service.

## Customize

Open `main.go` — it is fully commented. Start with the persona `BasePrompt`, then
replace the `greet` tool with something your bot should actually do. Add more
tools with additional `WithTypedTool(...)` options.
